<%#
  EJECTED — the assessment window was left

  Route:  GET /assessment/ejected
  Layout: nav: 'none'

  Reachable without a session, because the session has just been destroyed.
  Carries no participant detail for the same reason — and because anyone who
  reaches this URL directly should learn nothing from it.

  The tone here is deliberate. The officer may well have done nothing wrong: a
  call came in, the screen locked, a notification stole focus. Accusing them
  would be both unpleasant and, often, incorrect. It states what happened and
  what to do next.
%>

<div class="container container-narrow section">

  <div class="card card-lg">
    <span class="label-meta">Session ended</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,32px);margin-bottom:var(--sp-3)">
      Your assessment window was closed
    </h1>

    <p style="font-size:16px;color:var(--text-muted)">
      The assessment must stay open in a single window until you submit. Your
      session has ended because the window was left or hidden.
    </p>

    <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert" style="margin:var(--sp-5) 0">
      <strong>Your answers are safe.</strong> Everything you had already
      answered is saved. You will return to the same assessment, not a new one.
    </div>

    <h3>What to do now</h3>
    <ol class="text-muted" style="font-size:15px;padding-left:var(--sp-5);margin:0 0 var(--sp-5)">
      <li style="margin-bottom:var(--sp-2)">Speak to the facilitator running the session.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:var(--sp-2)">They will issue you a new access PIN.</li>
      <li>Sign in again with your participant number, surname and the new PIN.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="text-faint" style="font-size:13px;margin:0">
      The ten-minute limit continues to run, so speak to the facilitator
      promptly. Your previous PIN no longer works.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="row" style="gap:var(--sp-3);margin-top:var(--sp-5)">
    <a href="/login" class="btn btn-terracotta">Return to sign-in</a>
  </div>

</div>
